---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import TopicList from '../../components/overview/TopicList.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../consts';

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;

const dosing = `// The causal chain: each step needs the result of the one before it.
let report = CausalFlow::value(MEASURED)
    .try_step(elimination_rate) // the patient's elimination rate; fails unless the levels decay
    .map(peak_at_end_of_infusion) // backward in time, to 08:30
    .map(trough_before_next_dose) // forward in time, to 16:00
    .map(dosing_interval) // the verdict and the interval
    .map(dose_for_interval) // the dose that holds the peak at the new interval
    .map(round_trip) // forward again from the peak: must return the 09:30 level
    .finish()?;

/// First-order elimination: the concentration \`hours\` after \`mg_l\`. The law is exact in either
/// direction of time, so a negative \`hours\` runs it backward.
pub fn concentration_after(mg_l: FloatType, k_per_h: FloatType, hours: FloatType) -> FloatType {
    mg_l * Real::exp(-(k_per_h * hours))
}`;

const dosingOut = `Regimen: 120 mg every 8 h, infused 08:00 to 08:30
Measured: 7.00 mg/L at 09:30 and 3.50 mg/L at 15:30

1. Elimination rate:  k = 0.1155 /h, half-life 6.0 h
2. Peak (backward):   7.86 mg/L at 08:30   target 6-10 mg/L: in range
3. Trough (forward):  3.30 mg/L at 16:00   target at most 2 mg/L: OUT OF RANGE
4. Interval:          a peak of 8 mg/L falls to 1 mg/L after 18.5 h; dose every 24 h
5. Dose:              volume 24.6 L; 190 mg every 24 h holds the peak at 8 mg/L

Check: the law run forward from the peak returns 7.00 mg/L at 09:30 (difference 0.0e0 mg/L)`;

const gentamicin = `${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/examples/medicine_examples/gentamicin_dosing`;
const sawchuk = 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/837654/';
const begg = 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2014209/';

const whatIf = `// The first ten minutes, as observed. Every run continues from this flow for the rest.
let at_branch = plug_in(full_flow_cooling()?)?.iterate_n(BRANCH_MINUTE, one_minute);
let finish = |run: Charge| {
    run.iterate_n(RUN_MINUTES - BRANCH_MINUTE, one_minute)
        .into_process()
};

let observed = finish(at_branch.clone());
// What if the cell had been at 52 °C after ten minutes?
let hot_cell = finish(at_branch.clone().alternate_value(HOT_CELL_C));
// What if the coolant pump had dropped to a quarter of its flow?
let weak_pump = finish(at_branch.clone().alternate_context(quarter_flow_cooling()?));
// What if the cell had held 75 % charge after ten minutes?
let full_cell = finish(at_branch.alternate_state(HIGH_CHARGE_PCT));`;

const fastCharge = `${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/examples/causal_counterfactual_examples/counterfactual_fast_charge`;
const correctionExamples = `${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/examples/causal_correction_examples`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Dynamic causality — DeepCausality"
  description="The causal monad composes a model on two layers: a one-directional causal order, and an unconstrained step that can run a law backward in time. Three channels, a counterfactual on each, an audit log, causaloids and the Causal State Machine."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Overview · Dynamic causality</p>
      <h1>A causal order with computation inside each step</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The causal monad in <code>deep_causality_core</code> composes a model on two layers. The
        outer layer decides the causal order. The inner layer, the step, computes
        anything, including a law run backward in time. Mid-run, a counterfactual can
        replace the effect, the context or the state.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Two layers of composition</h2>
      <p>
        The classical formulation of causality, "if A then B, and if not A then not B," composes on
        one layer: the causal direction from A to B doubles as the direction of time, so it cannot
        express a time-symmetric relation as a cause. That rules out the fundamental equations of
        physics, from Newton's through Maxwell's to Schrödinger's, which are all time-symmetric.
      </p>
      <p>DeepCausality composes on two layers.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The outer layer is the causal order.</strong> Steps compose by monadic bind: a
          step runs after the one before it and receives its effect, state and context, and an error
          stops the chain. A causal graph arranges steps into a directed acyclic graph by
          construction, so no step can feed back into an earlier one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The inner layer is the step itself.</strong> A step, an ordinary Rust function, can
          hold a time-symmetric law of physics, a law run backward to reconstruct the past, or any
          other computation. It assumes neither space nor time; location and time reach it through
          the context or as values such as the <code>hours</code> below.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Therapeutic drug monitoring shows both layers at work. Gentamicin, an antibiotic used
        against serious infections, kills bacteria in proportion to its peak concentration, and its
        toxicity to the kidneys and the inner ear follows total exposure. Under conventional
        multiple-daily dosing, a ward therefore checks both ends of a dose: a peak of 6–10 mg/L and a
        trough of at most 2 mg/L (<a href={begg}>Begg et al. 1999</a>). It rarely samples at either
        end. It draws two levels in between, and the method of <a href={sawchuk}>Sawchuk et al.
        (1977)</a> reconstructs the rest with one first-order elimination law.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href={gentamicin}>gentamicin example</a> writes that method as a causal chain. A
        patient receives 120 mg every 8 hours, infused from 08:00 to 08:30, and the lab measures
        7.0 mg/L at 09:30 and 3.5 mg/L at 15:30:
      </p>
      <Code code={dosing} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <Code code={dosingOut} lang="text" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The two levels give the patient's elimination rate: a half-life of 6 hours. The peak step
        runs the elimination law with a negative <code>hours</code>, from 09:30 back to the end of
        the infusion, and finds 7.86 mg/L, inside the target. The trough step runs the same law
        forward from that peak to the next dose and finds 3.30 mg/L, above the 2 mg/L that limits
        accumulation. At a 24-hour interval a peak of 8 mg/L falls to 1 mg/L first, and the
        patient's volume of distribution, 24.6 L, sets the dose that holds that peak: 190 mg once
        daily. The last step runs the law forward from the reconstructed peak and returns the 09:30 level
        exactly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each step follows the one before it in causal order: it cannot run until that step has
        produced its result, and an error stops the chain. The first step fails, for instance, on two
        levels that do not decay. In physical time the chain runs back and then forward: the peak at
        08:30 is computed from a level drawn an hour later. Drug elimination is not time-symmetric,
        but its law is exact in both directions, so one function serves both steps. In the classical
        formulation the arrow of cause is also the arrow of time. Here the causal flow carries the
        order and the elimination law carries the pharmacology, and the two arrows can point
        different ways.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same two layers hold a time-symmetric law of physics: the <a href="/examples/orbit-round-trip/">orbit round trip</a> propagates a satellite 600 s forward and 600 s back, and shows
        the round trip at four precisions, from <code>BFloat16</code> to <code>Float106</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Three channels and a log</h2>
      <p>Each step receives exactly three channels from the step before it.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Channel</th><th>Type</th><th>What it gives a model</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Effect</td><td>a value, <code>None</code> for "no evidence," or a <code>RelayTo</code> command that jumps to another node of a graph</td><td>the result of the previous step</td></tr>
            <tr><td>State</td><td>any type; <code>()</code> when unused</td><td>memory carried from step to step</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Context</td><td>any type, passed as <code>Option&lt;C&gt;</code></td><td>the environment: data, time and space, with fixed or changing relations</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Without state, a step depends only on the effect it receives. With state, a step carries
        whatever history the state type holds. The context can be as simple as <code>()</code> or as
        rich as the <a href="/overview/dynamic-context/">context graph</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The log sits outside the channels: bind appends each step's entries after the earlier ones
        and never passes the log to a step, so no step can read it. No method removes or edits an
        entry, and each entry carries a wall-clock timestamp. The log records what the model computed
        and when: provenance for debugging, and evidence where an auditor asks for it.
      </p>

      <h2>A counterfactual can replace any of the three channels</h2>
      <p>
        Each channel has its own counterfactual. A call replaces one channel of a running chain, the
        steps after it run the same law on the replacement, and the other two channels continue as
        observed.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Channel</th><th>Method</th><th>The question it asks</th><th>Log marker</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Effect</td><td><code>alternate_value</code></td><td>What if this result had been different?</td><td><code>!!ValueAlternation!!</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Context</td><td><code>alternate_context</code></td><td>What if the world had been different?</td><td><code>!!ContextAlternation!!</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>State</td><td><code>alternate_state</code></td><td>What if the history had been different?</td><td><code>!!StateAlternation!!</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each replacement writes its marker to the log. On a chain that has already failed, the call
        does nothing, so a counterfactual cannot hide an upstream error. No method replaces the error
        or the log.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href={fastCharge}>fast-charge example</a> asks all three questions of one model. A
        lithium-ion cell charges for 30 minutes, and each minute is one step: the cell temperature
        is the effect, the charge is the state, and the coolant is the context. The charger halves
        its current above 45 °C and stops above 55 °C. Above 80 % charge it holds the cell voltage,
        so the current tapers to zero at 100 %. The example observes the run for ten minutes, then
        branches it three ways:
      </p>
      <Code code={whatIf} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Run</th><th>Replaced at minute 10</th><th>Cell after 30 min</th><th>Charge</th><th>Next minute's current</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Observed</td><td>nothing</td><td>38.8 °C</td><td>70.0 %</td><td>50.0 A</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hot cell</td><td>effect: 33.5 °C → 52 °C</td><td>41.2 °C</td><td>65.8 %</td><td>50.0 A</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weak pump</td><td>context: coolant at a quarter of its flow</td><td>45.5 °C</td><td>65.0 %</td><td>25.0 A</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Full cell</td><td>state: 36.7 % → 75 % charge</td><td>30.3 °C</td><td>95.4 %</td><td>11.4 A</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>The hot cell</strong> starts minute eleven at 52 °C, so the charger halves its
        current. The cell cools below 45 °C within five minutes and full current resumes, but the
        charge lost in those minutes stays lost. The run ends 4.2 percentage points behind.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The weak pump</strong> leaves the cell's temperature and charge as observed and
        changes the world it charges in. With a quarter of the cooling, the cell warms past 45 °C at
        minute 23. From then on the charger switches between half and full current at that line. The
        run ends 5 points behind, 6.7 °C hotter, and at half current.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The full cell</strong> leaves the temperature and the coolant as observed and
        changes the history. At 75 % the cell reaches 80 % at minute 13, and the charger moves to
        its taper. The current falls, the cell cools to 30.3 °C, and the charge ends at 95.4 %. The
        same step runs a different part of the charging law because the state it carries is
        different.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each counterfactual run carries one log entry: the value entry names the 33.5 °C the model
        had computed and the 52 °C that replaced it, and the other two record which channel changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pearl's structural causal models frame a counterfactual as setting a variable, after first
        inferring the background conditions of the factual world from evidence. A
        <code>PropagatingProcess</code> carries the context and the state as channels of the
        running chain, so the branch point already holds the factual world and its history. A
        counterfactual replaces one of them with a single call.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/examples/pearl-counterfactual/">Pearl's ladder example</a> walks association,
        intervention and counterfactual with <code>alternate_value</code>. The <a href={correctionExamples}>correction examples</a> call <code>alternate_value</code> inside
        a running loop: each step, a monitor checks the result against a threshold in the context,
        and on an anomaly replaces it with a correction the run continues from.
      </p>

      <h2>Causaloids: one step, many steps, a graph</h2>
      <p>
        A causaloid packages a causal function with an identity, in one of three forms.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Singleton.</strong> Runs one function and logs its input and output.</li>
        <li><strong>Collection.</strong> Feeds one input to every member and aggregates the results with <code>All</code>, <code>Any</code>, <code>None</code> or <code>Some(k)</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Graph.</strong> Evaluates a directed acyclic graph of causaloids in topological order. Where branches meet, it joins their effects: logical OR for booleans, the maximum for numbers. A <code>RelayTo</code> command restarts evaluation at the node it names.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <code>Causaloid::evaluate</code> runs a singleton. A collection or a graph runs through its own
        engine, <code>evaluate_collection</code> or <code>evaluate_subgraph_from_cause</code>, which a
        bind step can call. The stateful graph engine threads state along each path and returns an
        error at a join, where two states would have to merge. The <a href="/examples/aerospace-flight-envelope/">flight envelope monitor</a> uses all three
        shapes inside one stateful chain: a collection of sensor-health checks, a bind chain for state
        estimation, and a graph of envelope protections.
      </p>

      <h2>From a result to an action</h2>
      <p>
        The Causal State Machine pairs each state with an action. Each state holds a causaloid, and
        the machine fires the paired action when the causaloid's result is active. The <a href="/overview/dynamic-action/">dynamic action</a> page covers the machine.
      </p>

      <h2>What the monad leaves to you</h2>
      <p>
        Bind accepts any Rust closure as a step, so the monad cannot guarantee that a step is pure: a
        closure may read a clock, do IO or capture a variable. A causaloid narrows this by storing a
        function pointer, which cannot capture its environment. Because bind accepts any function of
        the right type, the model author decides which dependencies count as causal.
      </p>

      <h2>Machine-checked laws</h2>
      <p>
        The monad and the causaloid engine carry Lean 4 proofs: the monad laws, the structure of the
        error-and-log stack, termination of relay commands, closure of the verdict algebra, and order
        invariance of the graph join. Each theorem has one Rust test that pins the implementation to
        the same statement. The <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/lean/THEOREM_MAP.md`}>theorem
        map</a> links every proof to its test.
      </p>

      <TopicList current="dynamic-causality" />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
